A||||6||PAUL88GT||Preparation for paint: a few questions||||1||
Z||000000||PAUL88GT||11-28-2001||07:26 PM||||I have been prepping my 86SE for new paint and have a few questions.  I plan to let Maaco spray the car after I do all the prep work. Our Maaco has a good reputation and I plan to use the highest cost process.  I am going with all Black as the color.<P>Here is what I have been doing:<P>1) Sanding with 320 grit using a DA electric sander.  I have removed all the gloss and I have taken the front and rear facias down to the bare plastic because of the spider webs in the paint<BR>2) I have spot filled all the nicks with Kombi putty and sanded them smooth with 320 grit.<P>My questions relate to the final steps.<P>1) Should I put primer over the entire car or just the areas that are sanded down to bare plastic. What brand of rattle-can primer should I use.<P>2) I plan to sand around the grooved trim with a scotch pad.  Should I have Maaco paint the trim when they paint the car.  Will the paint stick to the plastic trim?<P>3) I plan to wet sand with 400 grit as the final step after the primer has been sprayed on. Is this correct?  <P>4) The paint process the guy at Maaco described is two coats (combination clear coat and base coat).  Should I request separate base and clear coats? If so, how many coats of each?<P>Sorry about all the questions, but this is first time I have prepped a car for painting.<P>Also, I plan to paint the following items off the car: headlight doors, spoiler, engine vents, mirrors, and the sunroof gasket will be removed as well.<P>TIA,<BR>Paul<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||72vinman||11-28-2001||09:04 PM||||Bump<BR>I am getting ready to do the same thing. I am also interested in any info.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigvin2_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||11-28-2001||09:05 PM||||Well, I'm not a paint expert, but I would have to say if you want a better job, it is better to take off the door mouldings.  Paint might stick to them, but it will look like crappy prep work.  It is more difficult, as well as more money to do, but it would look better in the end.  But, whatever you want.  Just a suggestion.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mgbringo||11-28-2001||10:57 PM||||I am an amatuer painter.  I have painted about 25 cars over the past several years, mostly mine.  Not a professional but each one turned out better than the last one.  Here are my answers for what they are worth;<P>1) Sanding with 320 grit using a DA electric sander. I have removed all the gloss and I have taken the front and rear facias down to the bare plastic because of the spider webs in the paint<P>Sounds good.  Just make sure EVERY last piece of paint has been sanded/scuffed up.  If it is not, paint will peal from it in the future.  Spend as much time getting the edges as you did the rest of the car.    I would also wash it with some Dawn dish washing liquid in a bucket of water to remove all the wax and grease.<P>2) I have spot filled all the nicks with Kombi putty and sanded them smooth with 320 grit.  <P>Make sure all putty is feather edged.  That means it looks like a feather at the edges, which means it is smooth.  Use a flat sanding block.<P>My questions relate to the final steps.<P>1) Should I put primer over the entire car or just the areas that are sanded down to bare plastic. What brand of rattle-can primer should I use.<P>My prefered method is to prine the entire car with a filler primer.  It fills the imperfections.  You then sand it by hand, no power sanders, with 320, then 400 or 600, all with plenty of water.  If you have to use rattle cans use Krylon.  It is my favorite.  Then tell the painter what you used.  The paint he uses must be compatible with it.  Incompatible primer and paint will be a real mess. It has happened to me.<P>2) I plan to sand around the grooved trim with a scotch pad. Should I have Maaco paint the trim when they paint the car. Will the paint stick to the plastic trim?<P>Be sure you get every last area sanded well.  So the paint sticks.  This is usually missed by amatuer painters and then the new paint peels from around the trim.  I would also sand the trim and let them paint it.  Sand it good though, all edges.<P>3) I plan to wet sand with 400 grit as the final step after the primer has been sprayed on. Is this correct? <P>Yes that will work.  400 or 600 or both by hand with a flat block sander.  <P>4) The paint process the guy at Maaco described is two coats (combination clear coat and base coat). Should I request separate base and clear coats? If so, how many coats of each?<P>He is probably just spraying it with two coats of enamel.  That is OK, if you go that way ask for 3 or 4 coats so you can color sand it.  When I want a top notch paint job I use seperate base coat and then clear coat.  It will last longer and look better.  It will cost a lot more though.  Just materials for the last car I painted with base coat clear coat was $900.<P>The more coats of paint or clear you use the more you can color sand it to get it looking like a mirror.  Color sanding is a ton of work but you will be happy with the results.  You sand it by HAND with that flat sanding block with 1000, then 1500, then 2000 grit paper and then buff it out with 10,000 grit polishing compound and a FOAM pad.  It will come out like a mirror with the best shine you have seen.  Have to be careful though to not sand through the paint/clear coat.  Don't sand on the edges as it will go through very quickly.  Use a SLOW rotating polisher.  A drill at full speed will burn the paint and ruin it.  Use a polisher or a variable speed drill on very slow speed and move around a lot.<P>If you have any more questions, ask away.<P>Ron<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fierobruiser||11-28-2001||10:58 PM||||My 88 was repainted black by the previous owner. Probably through laziness, judging by the sections of the car that were missed, he did not remove the hip moldings. I think that they look better painted especially on a black car then left in the unpainted finish. It seems to stick alright to the plastic moldings, although I was able to remove some using yellow vinyl tape after an attempt to envision what the car would look like accented in yellow or gold. <P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex4mula||11-29-2001||08:46 AM||||Trim is not hard to remove at all. It just takes time. If you remove the wheel wells (god time to clean/refinish them) you can access the inner bolts for the front qtr panels and GT fascia. For the small one on the door (next to handle) try to remove the inner panel torx screws and pry it out to access. For the rest you carefuly pull up and to one side and they will come off. Take the car like that to the shop and you will have a better job.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
Z||000006||84Bill||11-29-2001||09:50 AM||||Paul <BR>Sounds good to me. I usually wet sand my primer with 400 let it dry for a day and wipe it down with naptha and a tak cloth just before I paint.<P>A good idea before you let macco take it is back tape the door handles, nosebadge anything you want to be sure not to get paint on. Also stuff news paper in the front door jamb around the hinges, raise the hood and backtape the headlight gaps to keep overspray out. just go mad with masking tape, it's cheap and well worth it. <P>I have worked in may shops and all the new guys do the tape work, no sense leaving it to chance  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
